I've often wished there were a spoiler along the lines of Joshua John
Buergel's "Which class should I (not) play?"
(http://www.helsinki.fi/~vviitane/spoilers/classes), but a) actually
up-to-date, and b) with more specific recommendations about play style
and the individual quests.  For instance, how many new players are
going to consider ditching a knight's starting armor, or know to try
for an insta-kill against the samurai quest nemesis?

Since I've never found such a guide, I've decided to start writing one
myself.  This is, of course, hampered by the fact that I haven't played
enough NH to have even seen most of the quests (I've seen A, and
completed the B & V), so most advice that I'd give at this point is
based on what I've read on RGRN and the spoilers.  Here's my start; any
comments/suggestions/corrections would be very appreciated; also, if
anyone wants to write up one (or more) of the classes that I don't have
much experience with, that would also be appreciated.

-=-=-=-
Barbarians: The basic hack-and-slash character class.  Given a good
weapon, barbarians can wade through the dungeon.  Low mental stats mean
limited spellcasting, if any, but with a big weapon, who cares?
Barbarians start with poison resistance, so the vast majority of
corpses are automatically safe to eat, which should make gaining the
other intrinsics easier (and dying of starvation harder).

The quest has many trolls, which will periodically come back to life,
which can be annoying.  The upper filler levels of the quest are large,
open, dark fields; bring along a light source, or finding the exits
will be a problem.  One good option is to read a scroll of light or zap
a wand of light at the stairs, as this will make finding them again
trivial.  The quest nemesis, Thoth Amon, has a weak attack, two magic
attacks, and a weak steal attack[1][2].

The quest reward, the Heart of Ahriman, is a luckstone and conveys
stealth, neither of which is too exciting (especially since you get
stealth at level 15, if you haven't already); it also has a damage
bonus when thrown, if you can bring yourself to do so, though this
seems like a desperate move. #Invoking it confers levitation, which can
be useful for crossing Medusa & Juiblex's levels, as well as the planes
of air, fire, and water; be careful, however, because the Wizard will
try to steal the Heart on your ascension run.

Valkyrie:
Valkyrie, like barbarians, are good hack-and-slash characters.  They
start with cold resistance and stealth, and get speed at level 7.
Valkyrie can only advance attack and escape spells to basic; generally,
their equipment will prevent effective spell-casting anyways, so don't
worry too much about magic.  Mjollnir is a guaranteed first gift from
#offering, but is a mixed blessing, since the lightning effect will
periodically shatter rings and wands that you would otherwise be able
to glean from your fallen foes; it may be better to use a different
weapon entirely.  With gauntlets of power, Valkyrie can throw Mjollnir
and have it return to them, but there's a 1% chance of it shattering
your own rings & wands; if you throw Mjollnir a lot, this will happen.
So don't.

The quest is largely populated by giants and fire ants, so picking up
fire resistance (if you don't have it by then) should be easy, as is
raising your strength to 18/**.  Beware of lava in the lower filler
levels; levitation is a good option, though it makes picking up items
more difficult.  The nemesis, Lord Surtur, has two moderately strong
attacks (2-20 damage), plus a weak steal attack[1]; if you've got the
hitpoints, he shouldn't be difficult.  He's also only resistant to fire
and stoning, so if you have Mjollnir by now, he should go down very
quickly.

The quest artifact, the Orb of Fate, is very nice for a melee
character--half damage (physical and magical), acts as a luckstone,
confers warning (you can sense monsters and their general power level
as they approach), and can be #invoked for level teleport (though you
probably want teleport control before doing this).  Oh, and it's a
crystal ball.  The downside?  It's heavy.

Wizards:
Wizards can be hard to get started, since they start off very weak (low
hitpoints, barely enough power to cast one spell, and low strength),
but they do have several advantages as well--they start with a cloak of
magic resistance, which is very valuable, and some rings and wands,
which can be very useful.  Gaining a couple of levels drastically
improves survival chances; high-level wizards have very little to fear
in the majority of the dungeon.  Smash every statue you find (force
bolt, which you start with, works well for this), as they will
periodically contain spellbooks.  Unlike all other classes, wizards are
warned if they may not read the spellbook correctly, so reading books
is not risky (as long as you're not attacked while reading).  One
caveat: don't read an unpaid spellbook in a shop, as that will cost 4/5
of the book's cost.  Wizards are guaranteed to get Magicbane as a first
gift, which is very useful--it doesn't dull when you engrave with it
and it does various things to monsters when you attack with it, all
good (for you).  It's a dagger-class weapon (athame), so many wizards
make getting expert in dagger a high priority.  Unlike most other
classes, #offering for artifacts isn't very important after the first
gift; it's probably better to #offer until it's safe to pray, and then
pray, in the hopes of getting a new spellbook.

The quest is largely populated by bats, birds, and xorns (the latter
can and will phase through walls to get to you, so be careful).  The
filler levels are standard room-and-corridor levels.  The nemesis, the
Dark One, has two weak attacks and a magic attack[2]; if you still have
your starting cloak, he shouldn't be too hard.

The quest artifact is the Eye of the Aethiopica--a souped-up amulet of
ESP.  Carrying it grants magic resistance (so it's redundant with your
cloak, but be aware that the Wizard will try to steal it), faster
energy regeneration, and half damage from spells; wearing it simply
confers ESP, so you're probably better off wearing something else.
#Invoking the Eye lets you gate between the various dungeon branches,
making getting to stashes easier; this doesn't work if you have the
Amulet of Yendor or have already left the dungeon.

